    {
      "id": "CNT-02",
      "chapter": "global",
      "title": "The index term in the mass comparison for a word",
      "anchor": "global-count-five-floor",
      "statement": "X_u^(fk)=Dstar/dstar_u equals the positive mass ratio times the computed index ratio times the product over places of the formal degree divided by the column Weyl dimension. Successive mass factors change the factorial product from rank k to rank r. In the large family the sharp log X_u floor is S + m log(g₀g₁) − 11L; the prime window then costs at most 2 on the log scale at its lower end.",
      "hypotheses": "Base volume equals genus mass at r; the positive Siegel mass ratio holds for the consecutive ranks; positive column dimensions and formal degrees. The sharp floor invokes DataA including separated weights, 44≤m≤k, π≤v, and scale/gap bounds. Γ>1 and log Γ/(fk)≤1 at the actual family point. The family threshold is chosen for fixed ε,C₀,C_Y before x,i,u.",
